Figure 11 presents H&E stained histology sections of muscle injection sites two weeks post-immunization, comparing aluminum hydroxide (AH) and Nano-11 adjuvants. The image demonstrates that Nano-11 causes significantly less residual inflammation compared to AH. The AH-injected site (left) exhibits extensive granulomatous inflammation (asterisks) with dense aggregates of macrophages and eosinophils. Conversely, the Nano-11-injected site (right) shows much milder inflammation, characterized by relatively few macrophages arranged in slender cords within the connective tissue. This indicates Nano-11 induces a transient inflammatory response that resolves more quickly than AH, supporting its potential as a safer vaccine adjuvant.